WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:01.180
- Let's face it,

00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:04.300
nobody really cares about
desktop computers anymore,

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:05.940
but that wasn't always the case.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:09.220
In 1998, Apple's mission
was to get people to care

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:12.430
about a little thing called the internet.

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:15.680
- We are targeting this
for the number one use

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.410
that consumer's tell us
they want a computer for,

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:20.010
which is to get on the internet.

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:23.070
- So Apple came up with
this bad boy, the iMac.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:24.960
(computer chimes)

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.680
Fun colors, fast performance,
and Jeff Goldblum.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:31.320
- Well, there's a computer
that makes it so easy,

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:32.460
you're up and running in 10 minutes

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.420
and you're still yourself.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:36.370
- But now, welcome to 2021,

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:39.860
where this new iMac turns
on in seconds, if that.

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.200
We care a lot about computers now,

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:44.750
just not the big, stationary kind.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:48.020
So you can say this 24-inch
iMac has a similar strategy

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:49.550
to that original one,

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:51.550
get people to care about desktops

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:56.100
with fun colors, fast
performance, no Jeff Goldblum.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.730
Believe me, I tried to get
in touch with him many times.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:01.620
Dear Mr. Goldblum,

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:04.240
will you be in my video?

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:06.360
Thanks, Joanna.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.530
With the new M1 chip, beautiful display,

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:10.350
and very good webcam,

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:13.780
this iMac is certainly more iPhone or iPad

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:17.400
than that 35-pound old monstrosity.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.470
But is that enough to convince
a laptop lover like me?

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:21.303
Let's find out.

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:25.550
Let's start with the design
because it's bonkers,

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:29.240
and there's good bonkers and
then there's bad bonkers.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:33.400
The entire computer sits in
this 0.4 inch thick rectangle.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:34.510
Just look at it.

00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:37.610
It's just a bit thicker
than an iPhone 12 Pro.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:39.810
Yes, everything that once sat in here,

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.850
processor, RAM, graphics, storage,

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:44.880
now sits in this thin shell.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.190
In fact, it's so thin,

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:48.520
they had to orient the
headphone jack this way

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.243
because there wasn't enough
room to do it this way.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:56.340
Like I said, bonkers, good bonkers.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.320
And this is all enabled
by a new M1 chip inside,

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:02.160
the same one inside the
new MacBook Air and Pro.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:03.990
Unlike Intel-powered machines,

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:06.410
this is based on the same arm architecture

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:09.430
found in chips in the iPhone and iPad.

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:12.530
It integrates the processor,
graphics, memory, and more

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:15.930
onto a single chip,
which means less space.

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:17.890
Some other things I like about the design

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:20.230
the power adapter magnetically
locked to the back,

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:22.010
there's an ethernet
port on the brick itself

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:24.300
if you want to plug
directly into the router,

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.510
and there's four USB-C ports.

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:28.690
Sadly, no SD card slot.

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:30.690
You know what reviewers
knocked this thing for?

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:32.330
No floppy disk.

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:34.310
It comes with matching accessories too.

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:37.410
You have a choice of the Magic
Mouse or the Magic Trackpad.

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:38.490
And just like the MacBook,

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:40.610
there's a fingerprint
sensor in the keyboard,

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:43.610
if you upgrade to the higher-end
configuration, that is.

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:47.040
But then there are a few
questionable design choices here.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:50.070
Like why is the screen
surrounded by this white frame?

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:51.990
I would much prefer it to be black.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:53.040
And I'm not the biggest fan

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.370
of the front pastel color options.

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:58.180
I'd much prefer the
darker color of the back.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:01.370
It's almost exactly like
Steve Jobs said back in 1998.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:02.550
- [Steve] The back of
this thing looks better

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:04.800
than the front of the
other guys, by the way.

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:09.320
- Plus, how the hell does this
mouse still charge like this?

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:11.240
Look, Apple, you know how to put a cable

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:12.390
at the top of a mouse.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:14.420
You did it once before.

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:15.970
What is this?

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:16.803
On the plus side,

00:03:16.803 --> 00:03:19.663
you get this very nice fabric
Lightening charging cord.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:22.130
Okay, let's talk about screens,

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:24.210
since that's what this thing really is.

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:26.960
The 24-inch panel has not a 4K display,

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:30.190
but what Apple calls a 4.5K display.

00:03:30.190 --> 00:03:31.100
Translation?

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:33.390
It's beautiful and crisp,
with plenty of room

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:36.180
for seeing multiple apps
open at the same time.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:38.140
Take that, 13-inch laptop!

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:40.730
Possibly my favorite part
of this whole machine?

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:42.830
The 1080P webcam.

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:45.610
Thanks to image quality
optimizations with the M1,

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:49.020
it completely shows up the
webcams on the new MacBook Pro

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:50.410
and even the front-facing camera

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:54.120
on the iPhone 12 Pro in
some lighting conditions.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:55.670
So how does this thing perform?

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:57.200
Just like the M1 MacBooks,

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:00.610
it's hard to get this thing to
slow down in everyday usage.

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:03.250
Web browsing with tons of
tabs while photo editing,

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:06.040
listening to music, and
watching a 4K video,

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:08.350
no trouble at all for this thing.

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:10.710
Wow, look at those graphics.

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:11.750
- [Computer] Checkmate.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:12.900
- The high-end model I tested,

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:16.290
which is priced at $1,899
with 16 gigabytes of RAM,

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:17.290
only started to chug

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:20.510
when I threw in big video editing
projects in Adobe Premier.

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:23.890
I do recommend the $200 upgrade
for the 16 gigabytes of RAM

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:27.000
for those who juggle a lot
of apps and web browser tabs.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.130
Sadly, you can't upgrade the RAM yourself,

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:30.920
like you were able to on this thing.

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:32.980
Those who want more power
and a bigger display

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:37.110
will have to wait until Apple
replaces the 27-inch iMac.

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.040
The company wouldn't
comment on future products,

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:40.430
but there are reliable reports

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:42.150
that it will get a more powerful M chip

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:44.760
and an even bigger high-contrast screen.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:45.750
So what do you do?

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:47.430
Well, I'd still get a laptop

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:49.720
for the freedom to move
away from this desk,

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:51.630
though I really wish Apple made a monitor

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:53.130
that looked like this.

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:54.040
But if you're in the market

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:56.900
for a very sleek stationary computer,

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:58.900
this iMac is very good.

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:03.900
It's GIF, GIF, GIF, GIF,
GIF, GIF...(audio fades out)

